<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kayseri</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Caesarea Mazaca, Mazaca</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kayseri is a city in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, at the foot of the extinct volcano Erciyes. As Caesarea Mazaca it was the capital of the kingdom of Cappadocia and the see of Basil the Great. Taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danishmendids">Danishmendids</a> and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> of Rum, it gained many mosques, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserais</a>, and it remains a major commercial centre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kayseri.jpg" alt="Kayseri" width="320" height="184" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carole Raddato from FRANKFURT, Germany · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mount_Erciyes_(Argaeus),_Turkey_(31870998063).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Towns such as Konya, <b>Kayseri</b>, Sivas, Aksaray and Malatya grew as centres of crafts and commerce, and the guild-like brotherhoods of the akhis organised urban artisans.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Assyrian merchants of the early 2nd millennium BCE carried it to Anatolia, where some 23,000 of their letters and contracts have been found at Kanesh, near present-day <b>Kayseri</b> in Turkey.</blockquote><a href="/language/akkadian">Akkadian · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>They built mosques and madrasas in Tokat, Niksar and <b>Kayseri</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/danishmendids">Danishmendids</a></li><li><blockquote>His realm included Sivas, <b>Kayseri</b>, Tokat, Amasya and Erzincan, with a Mongol and Turkmen military elite and Persian administration in the Seljuk tradition.</blockquote><a href="/state/eretnids">Eretnids</a></li><li><blockquote>Kadi Burhan al-Din Ahmad was a jurist from <b>Kayseri</b> who served the Eretnids as judge and vizier.</blockquote><a href="/state/kadi-burhan-al-din-state">Kadi Burhan al-Din state</a></li><li><blockquote>His adopted son Ariarathes II regained the land c. 301 BCE, and his heirs took the title king, ruling the plateau from <b>Mazaca</b> beneath Mount Argaeus.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-cappadocia">Kingdom of Cappadocia</a></li></ul></section>
